= The Burning of the Convents

Only weeks after the Republic was proclaimed, mobs across Madrid, Málaga, Valencia and the Andalusian towns set fire to convents, churches and religious schools. The immediate spark was a monarchist provocation, but the anger behind it is decades deep - the Church stood with the old order, the landlords and the dictatorship, and much of the urban working class has waited a long time for this.

Prime Minister Azaña utters the phrase that will follow him for years: that all the convents of Madrid are not worth the life of a single Republican. The <span style="color: #0087DC;">**CEDA**</span> and the monarchists seize on the disorder as proof that the Republic means godless chaos, and quietly more officers begin to listen to them.

[? if church_relation <= 0 : For the <span style="color: #c00000;">**PSOE**</span>, caught between a base that cheers the flames and a Republic that must be seen to govern, there is no clean position to take. ?][? if church_relation > 0 : We have tried to keep the Church on civil terms with the Republic; these fires make that work very much harder. ?]
